"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,229\nThe Learning Brain. Series 3: Generative AI\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,579\nConfident and wrong\n\n4\n00:00:12,379 --> 00:00:18,773\nTwo hundred and forty six people solved twenty logical reasoning problems from the law school admission test,\n\n5\n00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:20,653\nwith an AI assistant.\n\n6\n00:00:21,242 --> 00:00:24,845\nTheir scores went up by about three points over a norm group.\n\n7\n00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:29,354\nBut they thought they had done about four points better than they actually did.\n\n8\n00:00:29,524 --> 00:00:31,037\nNormally on this task,\n\n9\n00:00:31,087 --> 00:00:34,366\nthe weakest performers overestimate themselves the most.\n\n10\n00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:36,688\nThat is the Dunning Kruger pattern.\n\n11\n00:00:37,107 --> 00:00:39,061\nWith AI, it disappeared.\n\n12\n00:00:39,488 --> 00:00:42,608\nEveryone overestimated, by about the same amount.\n\n13\n00:00:42,778 --> 00:00:46,797\nAnd people who knew more about how AI works were more confident,\n\n14\n00:00:47,084 --> 00:00:49,802\nbut less accurate about their own performance.\n\n15\n00:00:50,192 --> 00:00:52,891\nThat is the opposite of what you might expect.\n\n16\n00:00:53,061 --> 00:00:57,382\nMost people sent a single prompt per question and accepted the answer.\n\n17\n00:00:57,881 --> 00:00:59,559\nWithout testing the reasoning,\n\n18\n00:00:59,789 --> 00:01:02,278\nthey got no feedback on where they were wrong.\n\n19\n00:01:02,756 --> 00:01:07,445\nAnd humans working with the AI did not beat the AI working alone.\n\n20\n00:01:07,615 --> 00:01:10,848\nA second study, with four hundred and fifty two people,\n\n21\n00:01:11,105 --> 00:01:12,532\nfound the same pattern.\n\n22\n00:01:12,702 --> 00:01:15,711\nThis is the metacognition problem from Series one,\n\n23\n00:01:15,792 --> 00:01:16,461\nsharpened.\n\n24\n00:01:17,005 --> 00:01:19,457\nFluent answers feel like understanding.\n\n25\n00:01:19,853 --> 00:01:22,371\nJudging your own accuracy is a skill,\n\n26\n00:01:22,575 --> 00:01:24,988\nand AI can quietly switch it off.\n\n27\n00:01:25,158 --> 00:01:25,956\nA caution.\n\n28\n00:01:26,330 --> 00:01:28,028\nThese are reasoning puzzles,\n\n29\n00:01:28,048 --> 00:01:30,259\nwith one model, in online samples.\n\n30\n00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:35,005\nBut they fit a wider finding: our feeling of knowing tracks fluency,\n\n31\n00:01:35,109 --> 00:01:36,302\nnot accuracy.\n\n32\n00:01:36,472 --> 00:01:37,237\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:37,623 --> 00:01:39,503\nBefore students see an AI answer,\n\n34\n00:01:39,523 --> 00:01:41,482\nhave them predict their confidence,\n\n35\n00:01:41,502 --> 00:01:42,286\nthen check.\n\n36\n00:01:42,846 --> 00:01:45,709\nAsk them to explain the AI's reasoning back,\n\n37\n00:01:45,729 --> 00:01:47,473\nor find one flaw in it.\n\n38\n00:01:47,872 --> 00:01:49,063\nAnd on some tasks,\n\n39\n00:01:49,083 --> 00:01:51,744\ngrade calibration, not just correctness.\n\n40\n00:01:52,064 --> 00:01:53,197\nMake it a routine,\n\n41\n00:01:53,217 --> 00:01:54,289\nnot a one off.\n\n42\n00:01:54,459 --> 00:01:57,134\nAI can make students smarter on the task.\n\n43\n00:01:57,519 --> 00:02:00,063\nOnly reflection makes them wiser about it.\n\n"
